AB  - This paper presents a novel approach for securing Vehicular Sensor Networks (VSNs) using a Post-Quantum Blockchain framework based on Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs). Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS) rely on Wireless Sensor Networks (WSNs) to support various technologies, such as infrared sensors, cameras, radar, GPS, IMU, LiDAR, engine sensors, weather sensors, and CO2 sensors. These technologies are integrated into autonomous vehicles for traffic monitoring. Hence, they become prone to WSN threats. Current cryptographic schemes are vulnerable to quantum computing, rendering traditional encryption methods obsolete. Moreover, the dynamic VSN environment requires efficient solutions that are adaptive to changing loads. To address these concerns, this paper proposes a blockchain-based architecture utilizing post-quantum mechanisms for encryption, authentication, and node and data validation to safeguard the integrity and privacy of Sensor data. Unlike traditional linear Blockchain, DAG allows for parallel execution of transactions, mitigating the computational burden posed by Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC). Furthermore, the paper explores the integration of Post-Quantum Proof of Work and Threshold Signatures to ensure secure and efficient consensus. Most complex computations are shifted from Sensors to the Gateways, reducing the overload. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed system can effectively handle large networks with enhanced security against quantum-based attacks, while maintaining scalability and system performance in dynamic environments. Β© 2026 IEEE.
