AB  - The development of a Quantum Internet that can scale needs to establish secure inter-domain communication methods which operate without needing any prior established trust. The majority of current proposals depend on either perfect quantum channels or a centralized system which quantum attackers can exploit. The paper presents a Quantum Web Protocol (QWP) stack which includes a hardware-based foundation for its development and a blockchain system which enables trustless decentralized access control. We developed four new standardized indices which connect quantum physics to network governance and successfully validated them through testing. Our system uses post-quantum CRYSTALS-Dilithium signatures from on-chain verification to establish trustless coordination which prevents unauthorized node access. The researchers evaluated system performance through a large-scale simulation which tested 100,000 trials under standard hardware noise parameters of T1 = 100 ΞΌs, 10-3 gate error. The system achieved a Route Success Rate of 72.0%, with an average end-to-end fidelity of 0.6137. The anomaly detector's built-in machine learning features demonstrated strong performance capabilities, achieving F1-Score = 0.7350 (Οƒ = 0.0932) and AUC-ROC = 0.9795 (Οƒ = 0.0096). The blockchain control plane processed over 1.5 million simulated transactions, which produced a cryptographic audit trail that could be digitally verified. The framework underwent comprehensive evaluation together with its measurement system, which will establish essential standards for developing future Quantum Internet systems. Β© 2025 IEEE.
