TI  - Design of a Blockchain-Driven Quantum Identity Model Using QHA-AC FQET and RL-QIDA Methods in Cloud Microservices
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 1st International Conference on Intelligent Computing and Systems at the Edge, ICEdge 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICEDGE67252.2025.11412890
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036703548?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Embedded Technology, School of Electronics Engineering, Vellore Institute of Technology, Vellore, 632014, India
AD  - Institute for Sustainable Industries and Liveable Cities, Victoria University, Melbourne, 8001, VIC, Australia
AB  - In recent, rapidly emerging cloud-native microservice architectures, there have been very strenuous requirements for maintaining secure, verifiable identity frameworks, but with quantum computing looming in the background, the requirements have become an even bigger challenge. Present models of identity in the edge environment are centralized trust dependency models and insecure static authentication mechanisms vulnerable to identity spoofing. To overcome these limitations, this study puts forward a Blockchain Driven Quantum Identity Verification Framework designed for microservice-to-microservice authentication between financial cloud infrastructures. This framework introduces quantum-resistant digital identities based on blockchain to enhance trustworthiness, adaptability, and confidentiality: Quantum Hash Anchoring with Adaptive Commitments (QHA-AC) utilizes quantum random walks to bind identities with temporal quantum hashes, which reduce the possibility of hash collisions to below 1e-18 and spoofing to 78%. Federated Quantum Entangled Tokens (FQET) enable decentralized mutual authentication through entangled quantum key pairs across cloud zones, effectively cutting cross-cluster ID spoofing by roughly 91%. Temporal Blockchain Quantum Graph Signatures (TBQGS) model service behavior using dynamic identity graphs verified through quantum gates and blockchain consensus, enhancing traceability with minimal overheads. Quantum Zero-Knowledge Identity Tree (QZKIT) constructs Merkle-like trees from fragmented quantum IDs validated through a zero-knowledge proof method, ensuring identity privacy with less than 0.5% leakages. Using Reinforcement Learning-based Quantum Identity Drift Analysis (RL-QIDA), deep RL is applied to monitor identity behavior in real-time with a 92.4% drift detection accuracy and a minimal false positive ratio. This framework improves authentication reliability and prepares infrastructures for the next-generation secure edge computing. Β© 2025 IEEE.
