AD  - Florida Polytechnic University, Lakeland, FL, United States
AB  - This chapter offers a comprehensive analysis of the cybersecurity challenges and solutions in smart grid systems, tracing the transformation from conventional centralized networks to resilient and adaptive cyber-physical platforms. It examines key architectural elements, including distributed energy resources, advanced metering infrastructure, operational technologies, electric vehicles, and cloud-based analytics, to assess how their rapid integration expands both system resilience and the attack surface. Using the Purdue model as a foundational framework, this chapter maps vulnerabilities across grid domains, investigates prominent attack vectors such as false data injection, ransomware, and supply chain compromise, and presents real-world case studies illustrating evolving risks. Defense mechanisms are critically evaluated, emphasizing cryptographic safeguards, robust authentication protocols, AI-powered and federated intrusion detection, digital twins, blockchain technology, and post-quantum cryptography. The discussion highlights the role and harmonization of leading international standards (including NIST CSF, NIST SP 800-82r3, NERC CIP, IEEE 1686 and 2030.5, DOE C2M2, and ISO/IEC 27019) as foundational instruments for risk management, compliance, and governance. The chapter concludes by surveying key emerging trends, such as 5G and 6G grid communications, privacy-preserving analytics, digital twin simulation, and international collaboration; these developments equip grid operators and researchers with the frameworks required to continuously engineer resilient and secure energy systems for the future. Β© 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
