AB  - Industrial Control Systems (ICS) and SCADA networks underpin critical infrastructure but remain vulnerable due to legacy protocols and growing IT/OT integration. Early designs prioritized real-time performance, leaving protocols such as Modbus, DNP3, IEC 60870-5, PROFINET, and IEC 61850 without authentication, encryption, or integrity protections. This review surveys protocol weaknesses, analyzes security standards, and highlights emerging solutions. IEC 62351 and IEC 62443 introduce TLS encryption, certificate-based authentication, role-based access control, and strong cryptographic mechanisms to enhance protocol security. Complementary defenses, including machine learning and digital twinβ€“based intrusion detection, offer process-aware anomaly detection beyond signature-based methods. Case studies of Stuxnet, Industroyer, and Triton demonstrate how adversaries exploit insecure protocols and supply-chain gaps, while also emphasizing effective mitigation strategies. Looking forward, blockchain, federated learning, zero-trust architectures, and post-quantum cryptography represent promising research directions. This review consolidates past challenges, present solutions, and future trends, offering a comprehensive perspective on securing ICS/SCADA environments. Β© The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
