AB  - As the world prepares for the arrival of quantum computing, numerous conventional cryptographic methods confront imminent vulnerabilities. The field of post-quantum cryptography endeavors to construct cryptographic algorithms that are impervious to the potential threats presented by quantum computers. The technology underlying cryptocurrencies, known as blockchains, heavily rely on cryptographic security and consequently assume a pivotal role in the quantum era. This scholarly article investigates the potential of post-quantum blockchain technologies and their implications in the realm of digital forensics applications. We initiate with a comprehensive overview of the fundamental concepts of post-quantum cryptography and delve into its applicability within the blockchain domain. The potential advantages of integrating post-quantum techniques into blockchain technologies encompass heightened security against quantum attacks, lengthened cryptographic life cycles, and the establishment of platforms that ensure forensic soundness. Nevertheless, along with these potential benefits, challenges also emerge. The increased complexity of post-quantum algorithms may result in lengthier transaction du-rations, augmented computational requirements, and issues pertaining to scalability. Moreover, from the perspective of digital forensics, the robustness of post-quantum blockchains may introduce intricacies in data retrieval, evidence authentication, and cross-jurisdictional investigations. The study assesses a variety of post-quantum blockchain projects currently under development, evaluating their strengths and limitations within the context of digital forensics. In conclusion, while post-quantum blockchains offer a new frontier of security and forensic reliability, their effective implementation necessitates a harmonious balance between cryptographic robustness, practicality, and the evolving needs of digital forensic practitioners. Β© The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2024.
